  <content>
    <section>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="315fcda6-6840-450e-b38b-54a5191793d4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>A new bicycle sells for $300. It is on sale for \(\frac14\) off the regular price. <emphasis>Select three</emphasis>
          expressions that represent the sale price of the bicycle in dollars.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.5.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answers are \(300 \cdot
        \frac34\), \(300 \cdot (1-\frac{1}{4})\), and \(300-\frac{1}{4} \cdot 300\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a2643161-4674-476c-9af6-f3c3460683d3" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Answer the following questions that would complete the table to show the value of the computer at the listed times.</para>
            <para>A computer costs $800. It loses \(\frac14\) of its value every year after it is purchased.</para>
            <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
              <thead>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Time (Years)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Value of Computer (Dollars)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry>
              0
            </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
              1
            </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
              2
            </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
              3
            </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
              \(t\)
            </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <para>
          The value of the computer, in dollars, when it is purchased. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 800</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.5.1 - 5.5.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 800.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="13f87be0-8a80-417a-9f62-110463a8b249" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The value of the computer, in dollars, after 1 year. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 600.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.5.1 - 5.5.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 600.
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="c445b5b4-32a6-42c8-9bad-d67e1e2d023a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The value of the computer, in dollars, after 2 years. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 450.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.5.1 - 5.5.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 450.
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="58ae276d-f55b-4e60-81ba-accf3ce2b944" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>A computer costs $800. It loses \(\frac14\) of its value every year after it is purchased. Which of the
          following expressions would represent the value of the computer, in dollars, after \(t\) years?   
            </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(800 \cdot \frac{1}{4} \cdot t\)</para>
            <para>\(800 \cdot \frac{3}{4} \cdot t\)</para>
            <para>\(800 \cdot (\frac{1}{4})^ t \)</para>
            <para>\(800 \cdot (\frac{3}{4}) ^ t\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(800 \cdot \frac{3}{4}^t\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.5.1 -5.5.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(800 \cdot (\frac{3}{4})^t\).
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="1094ca3f-7329-4662-8499-099b5c4151a2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Use your equation from question 3 to find \(v\) when \(t\) is 5. What does this value of \(v\) mean? </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>189.84; After 5 years, the computer’s value is $189.84.</para>
            <para>142.38; After 5 years, the computer’s value is $142.38.</para>
            <para>3,000; After 5 years, the computer’s value is $3,000.</para>
            <para>253.13; After 5 years, the computer’s value is $253.13.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! 189.84, After 5 years the computer’s value is $189.84.
      </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.5.5.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is a. 189.84, After 5 years the computer’s value is $189.84. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="d80f8315-e291-4120-a412-6bfeaa6dca0e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>A piece of paper is folded into thirds multiple times. The area, \(A\), of the piece of paper in square
          inches, after \(n\) folds, is \(A=90 \cdot (\frac{1}{3})^n\).
          <para>
              What is the value of \(A\) when \(n=0\)? </para>
          </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! 90. That is the area of the paper in square inches before it is folded.
      </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.5.5.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 90. That is the area of the paper in square inches before it is folded. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="81b511ca-5f03-4c52-8065-2b024384738b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>
            How many folds are needed before the area is less than 1 square inch? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 5.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.5.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 5.
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="32c9b4e3-f7b5-4014-9bed-935b8993e174" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The area of another piece of paper in square inches, after \(n\) folds, is given by \(A=100 \cdot
          (\frac{1}{2})^n\). What does the number 100 mean in this situation? </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>The area after the paper is folded </para>
            <para>The area before the paper is folded</para>
            <para>The number of folds to have an area of 0</para>
            <para>The growth factor of the situation </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The area before the paper is folded. The a value in y=abx is always the initial value</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.5.5.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: The area before the paper is folded. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="23d280d7-65f6-4046-9238-bb6ca7d2d349" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The area of another piece of paper in square inches, after \(n\) folds, is given by \(A=100 \cdot
          (\frac{1}{2})^n\). What does the number \(\frac12\) mean in this situation?
        </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>The area of the paper before it is folded </para>
            <para>The area of the paper after 1 fold</para>
            <para>The growth factor of the situation</para>
            <para>The initial value of the situation </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The growth factor of the situation.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.5.5.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: The growth factor of the situation. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="d2d1c923-8e73-4821-9a49-dff891107b2a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Lin has 13 mystery novels. Each month, she gets 2 more. <emphasis>Select three</emphasis> expressions that represent the total number of mystery novels Lin has after 3 months.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 13 + 2 + 2+ 2, 13 + 6, 19.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.5.1.</para>
            <para>The correct answers are 13 + 2 + 2+ 2, 13 + 6, 19.
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="288b6740-1876-48df-8dca-a56367467de6" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The graph shows the amount of a chemical in a water sample. It is decreasing exponentially.


          <para><figure><media alt="Graph of a function on grid, origin O. Horizontal axis, time, hours, from 0 to 8 by 1's. Vertical axis, chemical, milligrams, from 0 to 1,200 by 200's. Plotted coordinates as follows: 0 comma 1,000, 1 comma 800. The next three points labeled A, B and C, with 4 more points plotted."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_07_13_01.svg"/></media></figure></para>
        </para>
            <para>
            What are the coordinates of point \(A\)?
          </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\((0, 1000)\)</para>
            <para>\( (2, 640)\)</para>
            <para>\( (\frac{4}{5}, 1000)\)</para>
            <para>\( (1, 640)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! (2, 640).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.5.3.
      </para>
            <para>The correct answer is (2, 640).
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="3f0a3962-d731-4dc9-baa0-de9dd06f1046" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What are the coordinates of point \(B\)? </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\((2, 640)\)</para>
            <para>\((3, 512) \)</para>
            <para>\((4, 410)  \)</para>
            <para>\((\frac{4}{5}, 512) \)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! (3, 512).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.5.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is (3, 512). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="da68b5f8-880b-4df0-b8f6-95983e982cfb" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which of the following equations would represent the chemical, \(C\), after time, \(t\), from the graph?
          </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(C =1000+ \frac{4}{5}x\)</para>
            <para>\(C = 1000 \cdot \frac{4}{5} \cdot x\)</para>
            <para>\(C = 1000(\frac{4}{5})^x\)</para>
            <para>\(C = 1000+ \frac{4}{5}+x\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(C = 1000(\frac{4}{5})^x\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.5.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(C = 1000(\frac{4}{5})^x\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="9dd82cf6-bff3-43c6-8b0a-5b572baf32ba" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The graph shows the amount of a chemical in a water sample at different times after it was first measured.
          <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> statements that are true.

          <para><figure><media alt="Graph of a function on grid, origin O. Horizontal axis, time, hours, from 0 to 6 by 0 point 5's. Vertical axis, chemical, milligrams, from 0 to 2,400 by 400's. Plotted coordinates as follows: 0 comma 2,000, 1 comma 1,000, 2 comma 500, 3 comma 250, 4 comma 125."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_07_13_02.svg"/></media></figure></para>
        </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.5.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answers are a and d.
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="553cc311-8ae5-4f89-8b67-f436cc999aa8" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The height of a plant in mm is 7. It doubles each week. <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> expressions that
          represent the height of the plant, in mm, after 4 weeks.
        </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \( 7 \cdot 2^4\), \( 7 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2\). </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.5.1.</para>
            <para>The correct answers are \(7 \cdot 2^4\) and \( 7 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2\).
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
